(json - JSON serialisation and deserialisation for Vox.

 Two functions carry the whole library, and they are each other's inverse:

   'to json' of <anything>   builds the JSON text for a Vox value
   'from json' of <text>     builds the Vox value for a JSON text

 They are Python's json.dumps and json.loads in Vox's voice. Two more stand
 beside them:

   'to json with indent' of <anything> and <spaces>
                             the same text, laid out to be read
   'reads as json' of <text> is this text one complete JSON document?

 Those four are the library. Everything else in this file is a step of one
 of them - Vox has no visibility control, so the rest is exported too, but
 nothing outside those four is meant to be called.

 The shapes correspond one for one:

   JSON object   <->  map          JSON string  <->  text
   JSON array    <->  list         JSON number  <->  number or decimal
   true / false  <->  boolean      null         <->  nothing

 COMPACT OR LAID OUT. 'to json' writes the tightest document there is: no
 space anywhere that JSON does not require one. 'to json with indent' writes
 the same document with each member on its own line, indented by that many
 spaces for every level it is inside, and a space after each colon - the
 layout Python's json.dumps(indent=n) produces, so the two agree byte for
 byte:

   'to json' of person
     {"name":"Ada","tags":["x","y"],"here":{},"score":9.5}

   'to json with indent' of person and 2
     {
       "name": "Ada",
       "tags": [
         "x",
         "y"
       ],
       "here": {},
       "score": 9.5
     }

 An empty object and an empty array stay on one line, having nothing to put
 on a second. A width of 0 still breaks the lines, it just does not indent
 them; a negative width is read as 0. Both forms parse back to the same
 value, and whitespace between tokens is exactly what 'from json' is built
 to ignore.

 A JSON number becomes a Vox number when it is written as a plain integer
 of eighteen digits or fewer, and a decimal when it carries a fraction, an
 exponent, or more digits than a Vox number can hold. That rule is written
 where it is applied, in 'read a number'.

 WHEN A DOCUMENT IS MALFORMED 'from json' hands back nothing and raises the
 error flag. It never prints and it never exits: a library that writes to
 your output is a library you cannot use.

 ASK 'reads as json' FIRST when the difference matters. Two reasons, and
 the second is the important one:

   - nothing is also the honest answer for the document `null`, so the
     answer alone cannot tell a malformed document from a null one;
   - the error flag does not cross out of a shared object on vox 0.4.10. A
     .so carries its own copy of the Vox runtime, so the flag this library
     raises is not the flag your program's `On error` reads. Raised it is,
     and an `On error` does catch it when this file is pulled in as source
     with `see "./json.vox".` - but a program that links libjson.so will
     never see it. Until that is settled, this is the pattern that works
     everywhere:

       If 'reads as json' of source then,
         a value called settings is 'from json' of source.
       Otherwise, Print "{Program}: that was not JSON".

 'to json' has no such trouble: it answers the empty text on failure, and
 the empty text is not a JSON document, so the answer says it plainly.

 These are the malformations detected:

   - a document that runs out before its value is complete
   - anything after the value but whitespace (trailing garbage)
   - an object key that is not a quoted string, or a missing colon
   - a missing comma, or a closing brace or bracket that never comes
   - a raw control byte inside a string (JSON requires it be escaped)
   - an escape that is not one of \" \\ \/ \b \f \n \r \t \uXXXX
   - a \u escape with fewer than four hexadecimal digits
   - a lone surrogate: half a character with no other half after it
   - \u0000, which a Vox text cannot hold: see 'read a unicode escape into'
   - a number with no digits, a leading zero, or a bare decimal point
   - a number too large or too small for a decimal to hold
   - nesting deeper than 64 levels, in either direction

 Build and consume:
   vox json.vox --shared -o libjson.so
   see json version "0.1" from "./libjson.lib".)

Library json version "0.1".

(Raising the error flag is something Vox gives an operation, not a
 statement, so the library performs the one operation whose only job here
 is to fail: reading a number out of text that is not a number. It has to
 be the last thing done before returning, because the next arithmetic or
 collection read clears the flag again.)
To 'raise the error flag'.
  a number called 'a number this plainly is not' is "malformed" as a number.

(One byte onto the end of a buffer. Writing one past the end is what grows
 a dynamic buffer, so this needs no scratch of its own.)
To 'write the byte' with a number called 'the byte to write' and a buffer called output.
  set byte {output's size add 1} of output to 'the byte to write'.

(True when the byte is a space, a tab, a newline, or a carriage return -
 the four JSON allows between one token and the next.)
To 'is whitespace' of a number called 'the byte'.
  Return a boolean, 'the byte' is ' ' or 'the byte' is '\t' or 'the byte' is '\n' or 'the byte' is '\r'.

(--- Serialising: a Vox value in, JSON text out ---------------------------)

(json.dumps. Builds the JSON text for any value: a number, a decimal, a
 text, a boolean, nothing, a list, or a map, nested as deeply as you like
 down to 64 levels. A map's entries come out in the order 's keys gives
 them, which is the order they were inserted, so a document that went
 through 'from json' comes back out in the order it was written.

 This is the compact form - no space anywhere JSON does not require one,
 which is what a document on the wire or in a file should be. For a
 document a person has to read, see 'to json with indent'.

 A decimal is written the way Vox writes one: fifteen digits after the
 point, with trailing zeros trimmed but never the last. Two consequences
 worth knowing before you trust a round trip - a decimal needing more than
 fifteen places to be written exactly is written rounded, and one smaller
 than a thousandth of a millionth of a millionth is written as 0.0.

 Raises the error flag and answers the empty text if the value is nested
 deeper than 64 levels, which is also what a value holding itself does.)
To 'to json' of a value called data.
  Return a text, 'the json for' with data and 0 and false.

(json.dumps with indent. The same document as 'to json', laid out to be
 read: every member of an object or an array on its own line, indented by
 'how wide' spaces for each level it is inside, and a space after each
 colon. An empty object and an empty array stay on one line. A width below
 zero is read as zero, which breaks the lines without indenting them.

 The text this writes parses back to the value it was given, so a document
 laid out for a person to read is still a document a program can read.)
To 'to json with indent' of a value called data and a number called 'how wide'.
  a number called 'spaces per level' is 'how wide'.
  If 'spaces per level' is less than 0 then, set 'spaces per level' to 0.
  Return a text, 'the json for' with data and 'spaces per level' and true.

(What both of them do, once the layout is decided.)
To 'the json for' with a value called data and a number called 'spaces per level' and a boolean called 'it is laid out'.
  Create a buffer called output.
  a map called writer is {"depth": 0, "failed": false, "indent": 'spaces per level', "laid out": 'it is laid out'}.
  'write a value' with data and output and writer.
  a boolean called 'the value could not be written' is writer's "failed".
  If 'the value could not be written' then, 'raise the error flag', Return a text, "".
  a text called document is "{output}".
  Return a text, document.

(A newline and the indent for a line at this depth - and nothing at all
 when the writer is writing the compact form, which is what keeps one set
 of writers serving both.)
To 'break the line at' with a number called depth and a buffer called output and a map called writer.
  a boolean called 'it is laid out' is writer's "laid out".
  If 'it is laid out' is false then, Return.
  a number called 'spaces per level' is writer's "indent".
  a number called 'spaces to write' is depth multiply 'spaces per level'.
  'write the byte' with '\n' and output.
  Repeat 'spaces to write' times, 'write the byte' with ' ' and output.

(A laid-out document puts a space after the colon; a compact one does not.)
To 'write the space after a colon' with a buffer called output and a map called writer.
  a boolean called 'it is laid out' is writer's "laid out".
  If 'it is laid out' then, 'write the byte' with ' ' and output.

(One value onto the end of output, whatever it turns out to be. The writer
 map carries how deep we are and whether we have given up, so that a value
 that contains itself stops at 64 levels instead of running out of stack.)
To 'write a value' with a value called data and a buffer called output and a map called writer.
  a boolean called 'we have given up' is writer's "failed".
  If 'we have given up' then, Return.
  a number called depth is writer's "depth".
  If depth is greater than 64 then, Set writer's "failed" to true, Return.
  If data is nothing then, append "null" to output, Return.
  If data is a boolean then, 'write a boolean' with data and output, Return.
  If data is a number then, append "{data}" to output, Return.
  If data is a decimal then, append "{data}" to output, Return.
  If data is a text then, a text called words is data, 'write escaped text' with words and output, Return.
  Set writer's "depth" to depth add 1.
  If data is a list then, a list called items is data, 'write an array' with items and output and writer.
  If data is a map then, a map called object is data, 'write an object' with object and output and writer.
  Set writer's "depth" to depth.

(JSON spells its booleans out, where Vox prints them as 1 and 0.)
To 'write a boolean' with a value called data and a buffer called output.
  a boolean called 'it is true' is data.
  append "false" to output, but if 'it is true' append "true" to output.

(An array: its elements in order, commas between them and none on the ends.
 The writer's depth is already the depth of the elements, so the closing
 bracket goes back one level to line up with whatever opened this array.)
To 'write an array' with a list called items and a buffer called output and a map called writer.
  a number called 'how many' is items's length.
  a number called depth is writer's "depth".
  'write the byte' with '[' and output.
  For each position from 1 to 'how many',
    a value called held is element position of items,
    if position is greater than 1 then, 'write the byte' with ',' and output.
    'break the line at' with depth and output and writer,
    'write a value' with held and output and writer.
  If 'how many' is greater than 0 then, 'break the line at' with {depth subtract 1} and output and writer.
  'write the byte' with ']' and output.

(An object: its entries in 's keys order, each one a quoted name, a colon,
 and the value it holds. An empty object stays as the two braces, having
 nothing to put on a line of its own.)
To 'write an object' with a map called object and a buffer called output and a map called writer.
  a list called names is object's keys.
  a number called 'how many' is names's length.
  a number called depth is writer's "depth".
  'write the byte' with '{' and output.
  For each position from 1 to 'how many',
    a text called name is element position of names,
    a value called held is object's "{name}",
    if position is greater than 1 then, 'write the byte' with ',' and output.
    'break the line at' with depth and output and writer,
    'write escaped text' with name and output,
    'write the byte' with ':' and output,
    'write the space after a colon' with output and writer,
    'write a value' with held and output and writer.
  If 'how many' is greater than 0 then, 'break the line at' with {depth subtract 1} and output and writer.
  'write the byte' with '}' and output.

(Backspace and form feed are written 8 and 12 below rather than as
 character literals: Vox has a literal for \n, \r and \t, but '\b' is the
 letter b and '\f' is the letter f, which would compile and be wrong.

 A JSON string: the text in quotes, with the seven characters JSON gives a
 letter of their own written that way, every other control byte written as
 \u00XX, and everything else - the bytes of any letter above ASCII
 included - passed through as it stands. The forward slash is left alone:
 JSON allows it escaped or bare, and bare is what a reader expects to see.)
To 'write escaped text' with a text called words and a buffer called output.
  a buffer called letters is "{words}".
  a number called last is letters's size.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  a number called 'the letter it escapes to' is 0.
  'write the byte' with '"' and output.
  For each position from 1 to last,
    set 'the byte' to byte position of letters,
    set 'the letter it escapes to' to 0,
    if 'the byte' is '"' then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to '"'.
    if 'the byte' is '\\' then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to '\\'.
    if 'the byte' is 8 then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to 'b'.
    if 'the byte' is 12 then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to 'f'.
    if 'the byte' is '\n' then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to 'n'.
    if 'the byte' is '\r' then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to 'r'.
    if 'the byte' is '\t' then, set 'the letter it escapes to' to 't'.
    if 'the letter it escapes to' is not 0 then, 'write the byte' with '\\' and output, 'write the byte' with 'the letter it escapes to' and output.
    if 'the letter it escapes to' is 0 and 'the byte' is less than 32 then, 'write a hex escape for' with 'the byte' and output.
    if 'the letter it escapes to' is 0 and 'the byte' is greater than 31 then, 'write the byte' with 'the byte' and output..
  'write the byte' with '"' and output.

(\u00XX for a control byte with no letter of its own. Only bytes below 32
 reach here, so two hexadecimal digits are always enough.)
To 'write a hex escape for' with a number called 'the byte' and a buffer called output.
  append "\\u00" to output.
  'write the byte' with {'the hex digit for' of {'the byte' bit-shift-right 4}} and output.
  'write the byte' with {'the hex digit for' of {'the byte' bit-and 15}} and output.

(One hexadecimal digit for four bits, which is what a hexadecimal digit is.)
To 'the hex digit for' of a number called 'the four bits'.
  If 'the four bits' is less than 10 then, Return a number, '0' add 'the four bits'.
  Return a number, 'a' add 'the four bits' subtract 10.

(--- Deserialising: JSON text in, a Vox value out -------------------------)

(The reader is a map, because a map parameter is the caller's map: every
 step below can move it on and every caller sees where it got to. It holds
 three things - "position", the byte it is looking at, counting from 1;
 "depth", how many objects and arrays it is inside; and "failed", which
 once true stays true and stops every step that follows.)

(json.loads. Reads one complete JSON document and answers the Vox value it
 describes: an object becomes a map, an array a list, a string a text, a
 number a number or a decimal, true and false a boolean, and null nothing.
 Whitespace is allowed anywhere JSON allows it. If the text is not one
 complete document - malformed, cut short, or with anything but whitespace
 left over - the answer is nothing and the error flag goes up, though see
 the note at the top of this file about the flag and a shared object: ask
 'reads as json' when the difference has to be certain.)
To 'from json' of a text called source.
  a buffer called document is "{source}".
  a map called reader is {"position": 1, "depth": 0, "failed": false}.
  a value called held is 'read the whole document' with document and reader.
  a boolean called 'the document was malformed' is reader's "failed".
  If 'the document was malformed' then, 'raise the error flag', Return a value, nothing.
  Return a value, held.

(Does this text read as one complete JSON document? Asks exactly what
 'from json' asks, and answers yes or no instead of a value, so that a
 malformed document and the document `null` stop looking alike. Reads the
 document a second time, so ask it of text you doubt rather than of every
 text you have.)
To 'reads as json' of a text called source.
  a buffer called document is "{source}".
  a map called reader is {"position": 1, "depth": 0, "failed": false}.
  a value called held is 'read the whole document' with document and reader.
  a boolean called 'the document was malformed' is reader's "failed".
  Return a boolean, 'the document was malformed' is false.

(One value and then the end of the text. Anything left over but whitespace
 means the text held more than the one document it was asked for.)
To 'read the whole document' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a value called held is 'read a value' with document and reader.
  'skip whitespace' with document and reader.
  a number called 'where the reader stopped' is reader's "position".
  If 'where the reader stopped' is less than or equal to document's size then, Set reader's "failed" to true.
  Return a value, held.

(One value, whatever it turns out to be, with the nesting counted so that a
 document built to exhaust the stack is refused instead.)
To 'read a value' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a boolean called 'we have given up' is reader's "failed".
  If 'we have given up' then, Return a value, nothing.
  a number called depth is reader's "depth".
  If depth is greater than 64 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  Set reader's "depth" to depth add 1.
  a value called held is 'read whatever is next' with document and reader.
  Set reader's "depth" to depth.
  Return a value, held.

(Which of the six kinds of JSON value is coming is settled by its first
 byte, and JSON is designed so that one byte is always enough.)
To 'read whatever is next' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  'skip whitespace' with document and reader.
  a number called 'the byte' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '{' then, Return 'read an object' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '[' then, Return 'read an array' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '"' then, Return 'read a text' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is 't' or 'the byte' is 'f' or 'the byte' is 'n' then, Return 'read a keyword' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '-' then, Return 'read a number' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is greater than or equal to '0' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to '9' then, Return 'read a number' with document and reader.
  Set reader's "failed" to true.
  Return a value, nothing.

(The byte the reader is looking at, or 0 once the document has run out.)
To 'the byte at' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a number called position is reader's "position".
  If position is less than 1 then, Return a number, 0.
  If position is greater than document's size then, Return a number, 0.
  Return a number, byte position of document.

(Move the reader on by one byte.)
To 'step past the byte' with a map called reader.
  a number called position is reader's "position".
  Set reader's "position" to position add 1.

(Move the reader on past whatever space, tab, newline, and carriage return
 it is standing in. JSON allows those four between any two tokens.)
To 'skip whitespace' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a number called position is reader's "position".
  a number called last is document's size.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  a boolean called 'still on whitespace' is true.
  While 'still on whitespace' and position is less than or equal to last,
    set 'the byte' to byte position of document,
    set 'still on whitespace' to 'is whitespace' of 'the byte',
    if 'still on whitespace' then, increment position..
  Set reader's "position" to position.

(Does the document read `word` where the reader is standing? If it does the
 reader steps past it; if it does not the reader has not moved, so a caller
 can try another word.)
To 'the document reads' with a buffer called document and a map called reader and a text called word.
  a buffer called wanted is "{word}".
  a number called 'letters wanted' is wanted's size.
  a number called position is reader's "position".
  a number called 'letters left' is {document's size subtract position} add 1.
  If 'letters left' is less than 'letters wanted' then, Return a boolean, false.
  a number called 'letters matched' is 0.
  a boolean called 'still matching' is true.
  a number called 'the byte found' is 0.
  a number called 'the byte wanted' is 0.
  While 'still matching' and 'letters matched' is less than 'letters wanted',
    set 'the byte found' to byte {position add 'letters matched'} of document,
    set 'the byte wanted' to byte {'letters matched' add 1} of wanted,
    set 'still matching' to false,
    if 'the byte found' is 'the byte wanted' then, set 'still matching' to true.
    if 'still matching' then, increment 'letters matched'..
  If 'still matching' then, Set reader's "position" to position add 'letters matched'.
  Return a boolean, 'still matching'.

(true, false, and null are the only bare words JSON has, and they are
 spelt in lower case only.)
To 'read a keyword' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a boolean called 'it says true' is 'the document reads' with document and reader and "true".
  If 'it says true' then, Return a value, true.
  a boolean called 'it says false' is 'the document reads' with document and reader and "false".
  If 'it says false' then, Return a value, false.
  a boolean called 'it says null' is 'the document reads' with document and reader and "null".
  If 'it says null' then, Return a value, nothing.
  Set reader's "failed" to true.
  Return a value, nothing.

(An object: pairs of a quoted name and a value, commas between them, until
 the closing brace. The reader is standing on the opening brace.)
To 'read an object' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a map called object is {}.
  a number called 'the separator' is 0.
  a boolean called 'there are more pairs' is true.
  'step past the byte' with reader.
  'skip whitespace' with document and reader.
  a number called 'the byte' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '}' then, 'step past the byte' with reader, Return a value, object.
  While 'there are more pairs',
    'read a pair into' with object and document and reader,
    set 'there are more pairs' to false,
    set 'the separator' to 0,
    if reader's "failed" is false then, 'skip whitespace' with document and reader, set 'the separator' to 'the byte at' with document and reader, 'step past the byte' with reader.
    if 'the separator' is ',' then, set 'there are more pairs' to true..
  If 'the separator' is not '}' then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  Return a value, object.

(One "name": value pair, added to the object. A name has to be a quoted
 string - JSON has no bare keys - and a colon has to follow it.)
To 'read a pair into' with a map called object and a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  'skip whitespace' with document and reader.
  a number called 'the byte' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is not '"' then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  a value called name is 'read a text' with document and reader.
  a boolean called 'the name was unreadable' is reader's "failed".
  If 'the name was unreadable' then, Return.
  a text called key is name.
  'skip whitespace' with document and reader.
  set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is not ':' then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  'step past the byte' with reader.
  a value called held is 'read a value' with document and reader.
  a boolean called 'the value was unreadable' is reader's "failed".
  If 'the value was unreadable' then, Return.
  Set object's "{key}" to held.

(An array: values with commas between them, until the closing bracket. The
 reader is standing on the opening bracket.)
To 'read an array' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a list called items is [].
  a number called 'the separator' is 0.
  a boolean called 'there are more elements' is true.
  a value called held is nothing.
  'step past the byte' with reader.
  'skip whitespace' with document and reader.
  a number called 'the byte' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is ']' then, 'step past the byte' with reader, Return a value, items.
  While 'there are more elements',
    set held to 'read a value' with document and reader,
    set 'there are more elements' to false,
    set 'the separator' to 0,
    if reader's "failed" is false then, append held to items, 'skip whitespace' with document and reader, set 'the separator' to 'the byte at' with document and reader, 'step past the byte' with reader.
    if 'the separator' is ',' then, set 'there are more elements' to true..
  If 'the separator' is not ']' then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  Return a value, items.

(A string: everything between the quotes, with each escape turned back into
 what it stands for. A byte below 32 inside a string is malformed - JSON
 requires a control character be written as an escape - and that is also
 how a document that stops in the middle of a string is caught, since a
 document that has run out reads as the byte 0.)
To 'read a text' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  Create a buffer called letters.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  a boolean called 'inside the quotes' is true.
  a boolean called 'the text is broken' is false.
  a boolean called 'the byte stands for itself' is false.
  'step past the byte' with reader.
  While 'inside the quotes' and 'the text is broken' is false,
    set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader,
    set 'the byte stands for itself' to false,
    if 'the byte' is less than 32 then, set 'the text is broken' to true.
    if 'the byte' is '"' then, 'step past the byte' with reader, set 'inside the quotes' to false.
    if 'the byte' is '\\' then, 'read an escape into' with letters and document and reader, set 'the text is broken' to reader's "failed".
    if 'the byte' is greater than 31 and 'the byte' is not '"' and 'the byte' is not '\\' then, set 'the byte stands for itself' to true.
    if 'the byte stands for itself' then, 'write the byte' with 'the byte' and letters, 'step past the byte' with reader..
  If 'the text is broken' then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  a text called words is "{letters}".
  Return a value, words.

(The reader is standing on a backslash. JSON gives eight characters a
 letter of their own and everything else the \uXXXX form; anything after
 the backslash that is neither is malformed.)
To 'read an escape into' with a buffer called letters and a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  'step past the byte' with reader.
  a number called 'the escape' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  'step past the byte' with reader.
  If 'the escape' is '"' then, 'write the byte' with '"' and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is '\\' then, 'write the byte' with '\\' and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is '/' then, 'write the byte' with '/' and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is 'b' then, 'write the byte' with 8 and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is 'f' then, 'write the byte' with 12 and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is 'n' then, 'write the byte' with '\n' and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is 'r' then, 'write the byte' with '\r' and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is 't' then, 'write the byte' with '\t' and letters, Return.
  If 'the escape' is 'u' then, 'read a unicode escape into' with letters and document and reader, Return.
  Set reader's "failed" to true.

(\uXXXX names a character by its number, and the reader has just stepped
 past the u. A character above U+FFFF has no four-digit number, so JSON
 writes it as two escapes in a row - a leading half from D800 to DBFF and
 a trailing half from DC00 to DFFF - which this puts back together. Half a
 character on its own, either half, is malformed.

 \u0000 is refused, though JSON allows it. A Vox text ends at its first
 zero byte, so a text holding U+0000 would come back cut short at it, and
 every character after it would be gone with no sign that it had ever been
 there. Refusing the document says so; truncating it would not.)
To 'read a unicode escape into' with a buffer called letters and a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a number called codepoint is 'the four hex digits at' with document and reader.
  If codepoint is less than 0 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  If codepoint is 0 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  If codepoint is greater than or equal to 0xDC00 and codepoint is less than or equal to 0xDFFF then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  a boolean called 'it is the leading half' is false.
  If codepoint is greater than or equal to 0xD800 and codepoint is less than or equal to 0xDBFF then, set 'it is the leading half' to true.
  If 'it is the leading half' is false then, 'write the utf8 for' with codepoint and letters, Return.
  a boolean called 'the other half follows' is 'the document reads' with document and reader and "\\u".
  If 'the other half follows' is false then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  a number called 'the trailing half' is 'the four hex digits at' with document and reader.
  If 'the trailing half' is less than 0xDC00 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  If 'the trailing half' is greater than 0xDFFF then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return.
  a number called 'the whole character' is 0x10000 add {{codepoint subtract 0xD800} multiply 1024} add {'the trailing half' subtract 0xDC00}.
  'write the utf8 for' with 'the whole character' and letters.

(Four hexadecimal digits read as one number, the reader stepping past each.
 Answers -1 when four of them are not there, which is how a \u escape cut
 short is caught. The digits are gathered and then cast, because base
 sixteen is a reading Vox already does: 'as a hex number' is the same cast
 that reads the exponent in 'the exponent at', told which base to read in.
 The gathering still has to say which bytes are hexadecimal digits, since
 that cast stops at the first byte that is not one rather than refusing
 the escape.)
To 'the four hex digits at' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  Create a buffer called digits.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  a boolean called 'still on a hex digit' is true.
  While 'still on a hex digit' and digits's size is less than 4,
    set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader,
    set 'still on a hex digit' to false,
    if 'the byte' is greater than or equal to '0' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to '9' then, set 'still on a hex digit' to true.
    if 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 'a' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 'f' then, set 'still on a hex digit' to true.
    if 'the byte' is greater than or equal to 'A' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to 'F' then, set 'still on a hex digit' to true.
    if 'still on a hex digit' then, 'write the byte' with 'the byte' and digits, 'step past the byte' with reader..
  If 'still on a hex digit' is false then, Return a number, -1.
  a text called figures is "{digits}".
  Return a number, figures as a hex number.

(A character's number written out as UTF-8, which is how Vox holds text:
 one byte below 128, and two, three, or four as it grows.)
To 'write the utf8 for' with a number called codepoint and a buffer called letters.
  If codepoint is less than 0x80 then, 'write the byte' with codepoint and letters, Return.
  If codepoint is less than 0x800 then, 'write the byte' with {0xC0 bit-or {codepoint bit-shift-right 6}} and letters, 'write the byte' with {0x80 bit-or {codepoint bit-and 0x3F}} and letters, Return.
  If codepoint is less than 0x10000 then, 'write the byte' with {0xE0 bit-or {codepoint bit-shift-right 12}} and letters, 'write the byte' with {0x80 bit-or {{codepoint bit-shift-right 6} bit-and 0x3F}} and letters, 'write the byte' with {0x80 bit-or {codepoint bit-and 0x3F}} and letters, Return.
  'write the byte' with {0xF0 bit-or {codepoint bit-shift-right 18}} and letters.
  'write the byte' with {0x80 bit-or {{codepoint bit-shift-right 12} bit-and 0x3F}} and letters.
  'write the byte' with {0x80 bit-or {{codepoint bit-shift-right 6} bit-and 0x3F}} and letters.
  'write the byte' with {0x80 bit-or {codepoint bit-and 0x3F}} and letters.

(A number, by JSON's grammar and not by anything looser: an optional minus,
 then either a single 0 or a digit from 1 to 9 with more behind it, then an
 optional fraction, then an optional exponent. A leading zero, a bare
 point, and a lone minus are all malformed, exactly as the grammar says.

 WHICH VOX TYPE IT BECOMES. A number written as a plain integer of eighteen
 digits or fewer becomes a Vox number, exactly. Eighteen is the last width
 at which every integer fits: nineteen digits reach past what a Vox number
 can hold, and would wrap round silently. Everything else - a fraction, an
 exponent, or a longer integer - becomes a decimal, and a decimal keeps
 about eighteen significant digits, so a longer number is the nearest
 decimal to what was written rather than what was written.

 The exponent is applied by moving the point rather than by multiplying, so
 1e2 reads as exactly 100.0 and not as 100.00000000000001. A number whose
 point would move more than 400 places either way is outside what a decimal
 can hold at all, and is malformed rather than quietly becoming zero or
 infinity.)
To 'read a number' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  Create a buffer called digits.
  a number called 'digits before the point' is 0.
  a number called 'digits after the point' is 0.
  a number called 'the exponent' is 0.
  a boolean called 'it is below zero' is false.
  a boolean called 'it has a fraction' is false.
  a boolean called 'it has an exponent' is false.
  a number called 'the byte' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '-' then, set 'it is below zero' to true, 'step past the byte' with reader.
  set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '0' then, 'write the byte' with '0' and digits, 'step past the byte' with reader, set 'digits before the point' to 1.
  If 'the byte' is not '0' then, set 'digits before the point' to 'take the digits into' with digits and document and reader.
  If 'digits before the point' is 0 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '.' then, set 'it has a fraction' to true, 'step past the byte' with reader, set 'digits after the point' to 'take the digits into' with digits and document and reader.
  If 'it has a fraction' and 'digits after the point' is 0 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is 'e' or 'the byte' is 'E' then, set 'it has an exponent' to true, 'step past the byte' with reader.
  If 'it has an exponent' then, set 'the exponent' to 'the exponent at' with document and reader.
  a boolean called 'the exponent was unreadable' is reader's "failed".
  If 'the exponent was unreadable' then, Return a value, nothing.
  a number called 'how many digits' is digits's size.
  a boolean called 'it is a plain integer' is false.
  If 'it has a fraction' is false and 'it has an exponent' is false and 'how many digits' is less than or equal to 18 then, set 'it is a plain integer' to true.
  If 'it is a plain integer' then, Return 'the whole number in' with digits and 'it is below zero'.
  a number called 'the point position' is 'digits before the point' add 'the exponent'.
  If 'the point position' is greater than 400 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  If 'the point position' is less than -400 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a value, nothing.
  Return 'the decimal in' with digits and 'the point position' and 'it is below zero'.

(Every digit the reader is standing on, written into digits; answers how
 many there were, which is 0 when the reader was not on a digit at all.)
To 'take the digits into' with a buffer called digits and a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  a number called 'how many' is 0.
  a number called 'the byte' is 0.
  a boolean called 'still on a digit' is true.
  While 'still on a digit',
    set 'the byte' to 'the byte at' with document and reader,
    set 'still on a digit' to false,
    if 'the byte' is greater than or equal to '0' and 'the byte' is less than or equal to '9' then, set 'still on a digit' to true.
    if 'still on a digit' then, 'write the byte' with 'the byte' and digits, 'step past the byte' with reader, increment 'how many'..
  Return a number, 'how many'.

(What follows the e: an optional sign and then at least one digit. Five
 digits is the most that can mean anything - a decimal runs out of range
 three hundred and eight places from the point - so a longer one is
 malformed rather than a number nobody can hold.)
To 'the exponent at' with a buffer called document and a map called reader.
  Create a buffer called digits.
  a boolean called 'it is below zero' is false.
  a number called 'the byte' is 'the byte at' with document and reader.
  If 'the byte' is '-' then, set 'it is below zero' to true, 'step past the byte' with reader.
  If 'the byte' is '+' then, 'step past the byte' with reader.
  a number called 'how many' is 'take the digits into' with digits and document and reader.
  If 'how many' is 0 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a number, 0.
  If 'how many' is greater than 5 then, Set reader's "failed" to true, Return a number, 0.
  a text called figures is "{digits}".
  a number called 'the exponent' is figures as a number.
  If 'it is below zero' then, Return a number, 0 subtract 'the exponent'.
  Return a number, 'the exponent'.

(The digits read as a whole number, with the minus put back on.)
To 'the whole number in' with a buffer called digits and a boolean called 'it is below zero'.
  a text called figures is "{digits}".
  a number called whole is figures as a number.
  If 'it is below zero' then, Return a value, 0 subtract whole.
  Return a value, whole.

(The digits read as a decimal, with the point dropped in at the position
 the exponent puts it. Writing the number out in full first and casting
 that is what keeps 1e2 exactly 100.0: the point moves, nothing is
 multiplied, and no rounding creeps in before Vox reads the decimal.)
To 'the decimal in' with a buffer called digits and a number called 'the point position' and a boolean called 'it is below zero'.
  Create a buffer called plain.
  a number called 'how many digits' is digits's size.
  a number called 'zeros before the digits' is 0.
  a number called 'zeros after the digits' is 0.
  If 'it is below zero' then, 'write the byte' with '-' and plain.
  If 'the point position' is less than or equal to 0 then, append "0." to plain, set 'zeros before the digits' to 0 subtract 'the point position'.
  Repeat 'zeros before the digits' times, 'write the byte' with '0' and plain.
  For each position from 1 to 'how many digits',
    if position is equal to {'the point position' add 1} and 'the point position' is greater than 0 then, 'write the byte' with '.' and plain.
    'write the byte' with {byte position of digits} and plain.
  If 'the point position' is greater than 'how many digits' then, set 'zeros after the digits' to 'the point position' subtract 'how many digits'.
  Repeat 'zeros after the digits' times, 'write the byte' with '0' and plain.
  If 'the point position' is greater than or equal to 'how many digits' then, append ".0" to plain.
  a text called figures is "{plain}".
  a float called 'the decimal' is figures as a float.
  Return a value, 'the decimal'.

